"question" "q-152132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{z}{5})]}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152132" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6d5a10153ce4677e9cadafa6ee5775de78829474fdca6d284f7beccb72de596d"} {"question" "q-152132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{20}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{5}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152132" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{20}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the substitution $u={x}^{4}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u={x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+9{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3u)]_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/94ad95c4f0477ae2dd29538e6cfcbbd52cbd7eda5b1c29246c1b4967566a7341.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+9{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152660" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6d5a10153ce4677e9cadafa6ee5775de78829474fdca6d284f7beccb72de596d"} {"question" "q-152660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{24}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152660" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi }{24}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-351761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-351761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/7151efaca7e4356e590a437f5d50a2ab6e5646e960b7ffa82b0bbed27bb9db47.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{8}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-351761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6d5a10153ce4677e9cadafa6ee5775de78829474fdca6d284f7beccb72de596d"} {"question" "q-351761" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-351761" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={t}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=3{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/c870dd367332455f2d4c35f85a188c64385a5195ca1058405a93f84c4ce31fd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})]-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6d5a10153ce4677e9cadafa6ee5775de78829474fdca6d284f7beccb72de596d"} {"question" "q-152667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3\\pi "] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-152667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-20502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-20502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={e}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/f55b37b456a6ab60c495c64be5f7afe510dc0889890ed2a070b371f9bbe69c1a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-20502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6d5a10153ce4677e9cadafa6ee5775de78829474fdca6d284f7beccb72de596d"} {"question" "q-20502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-20502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-20502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-347151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-347151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-347151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6d5a10153ce4677e9cadafa6ee5775de78829474fdca6d284f7beccb72de596d"} {"question" "q-347151" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "q-347151" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/state.json" "file_sha256" "f80f97e6d5da3d47fd38719f271772f3751a91191b1cd1f18702857ef5ae34ba"} {"question" "e-10687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10687.json" "file_sha256" "ee724e9e68e035027156d1cc13a8fce9e3fe33d072b8f11599c69d8b6802f9e8"} {"question" "e-10687" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =4\\cdot (0-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]) \\\\  & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y = \\arctan{x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/27ec65761e5119be666e69b67b2fa2fcb7b4f3665ddc41faddd6c136d5c955b1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-4{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)] \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10687.json" "file_sha256" "ee724e9e68e035027156d1cc13a8fce9e3fe33d072b8f11599c69d8b6802f9e8"} {"question" "e-10552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10552.json" "file_sha256" "4014d4c654c90c20e736026b4f2b57688c7091c927bdc60147c2fd1a40815c97"} {"question" "e-10552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)]}_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan{(4x)},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/9a7272f8bc8d0eba3e5bfc38e843cd02db3f3eb7b51e77b95b2efd61034890b6.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/4}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4a)]+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10552.json" "file_sha256" "4014d4c654c90c20e736026b4f2b57688c7091c927bdc60147c2fd1a40815c97"} {"question" "e-10551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10551.json" "file_sha256" "92f48ff4f5d067d13da35f0d43a2fc63f6c07370122d4ae73083b8012fcc028c"} {"question" "e-10551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})]}_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/042ad2450306552d9d5a7e4219d15547ad7104601b7ce6350a64193981de0de7.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})]-\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10551.json" "file_sha256" "92f48ff4f5d067d13da35f0d43a2fc63f6c07370122d4ae73083b8012fcc028c"} {"question" "e-10688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the substitution $u = x^2$ to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int}_{1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2x}{1 + x^4}   \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10688.json" "file_sha256" "a2c3c7fa4186e1c7ab5b836590b70e00db26c5cdfbbc3c679f70b5756f750081"} {"question" "e-10688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u = x^2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/assets/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039465/example-10688.json" "file_sha256" "a2c3c7fa4186e1c7ab5b836590b70e00db26c5cdfbbc3c679f70b5756f750081"}] "usage" {"e-10551" [] "e-10552" [] "e-10688" [] "q-6037" [] "q-152667" [] "q-20502" [] "e-10687" []} "basis" 1428 "retractions" [[17592186193196 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $∫_{0}^{∞}\\frac{2}{z^{2} + 9}dz$."] [17592186192947 :question/problem "What is $∫_{- ∞}^{1}\\frac{6t}{t^{4} + 3}dt$?"] [17592186193065 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u = e^{x}$ to evaluate $∫_{0}^{∞}\\frac{e^{x}}{1 + 3e^{2x}}dx$."] [17592186192282 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac{4}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186192282 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\arctan(x) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a \\to -\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}\\frac{4}{1 + x^{2}}dx &= 4 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)∫_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (x)]_{a}^{0} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (0) - \\arctan (a)] \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (0 - lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (a)]) \\\\\n&=-4lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (a)]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny = \\arctan{x}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{x}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/6c01ba2881dcb5e3c67965f2a0924e63.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{- ∞}^{0}\\frac{4}{1 + x^{2}}dx &= -4lim_(a → - ∞)[\\arctan (a)] \\\\\n&=-4(-\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192255 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1/4}^\\infty \\dfrac {2}{1+16x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186192255 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+(kx)^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(kx \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1/4}^{∞}\\frac{2}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx &= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{-1/4}^{a}\\frac{1}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{-1/4}^{a}(1)/(1 + (4x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[\\frac{1}{4}\\arctan (4x)]_{-1/4}^{a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (4a) - \\arctan (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] - (-\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] + \\frac{π}{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan{(4x)}\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan{(4x)}\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/6f825ead10f726d4996ce7df4d607c5f.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1/4}^{∞}\\frac{2}{1 + 16x^{2}}dx &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (4a)] + \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\frac{π}{2} + \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192254 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{2\\sqrt{3}}^\\infty \\dfrac{6}{x^2+4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186192254 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{k^2 + x^2} \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{1}{k} \\arctan \\left(\\dfrac{x}{k} \\right) + C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{∞}\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 2^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[\\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2})]_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2}) - \\arctan (\\sqrt{3})) \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)(\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2}) - \\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&= 3lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2})] - π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/cb07856ca51f2e4c4c57cae7ba76729b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{2\\sqrt{3}}^{∞}\\frac{6}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= 3lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (\\frac{a}{2})] - π \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2} - π \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192283 :question/problem "Use the substitution $u = x^2$ to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int}_{1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2x}{1 + x^4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186192283 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u = x^2$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{2x}{1 + x^{4}}dx &= ∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{2x}{1 + x^{4}}dx &= ∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (a) - \\arctan (1)] \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (a)] - \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arctan(x)\n$$\n\nshown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[\\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4005/Source/Images/e22f4e5f43eb489fe5da8cd1e73d5eab.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{∞}\\frac{2x}{1 + x^{4}}dx &= lim_(a → ∞)[\\arctan (a)] - \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186190532 17592186190533 17592186190534 17592186190535 17592186190536 17592186191170 17592186191171 17592186191172 17592186191173 17592186191174 17592186191965 17592186191966 17592186191967 17592186191968 17592186191969] "immutable_fields" [[17592186190531 :answer-field/correct] [17592186190531 :answer-field/id] [17592186190531 :answer-field/key] [17592186190531 :answer-field/type] [17592186191169 :answer-field/correct] [17592186191169 :answer-field/id] [17592186191169 :answer-field/key] [17592186191169 :answer-field/type] [17592186191964 :answer-field/correct] [17592186191964 :answer-field/id] [17592186191964 :answer-field/key] [17592186191964 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
